  "description": "A real Philippine Airlines e-ticket PDF came back with departureDate (from the barcode) but no returnDate at all, even though 05Jun2026 is clearly on the page. Two compounding root causes in the OCR-only fallback (the barcode only ever encodes the outbound leg, so returnDate always has to come from OCR): (1) this airline prints dates with zero separators anywhere, e.g. \"01Jun2026\" — the day-month-year pattern required at least one separator character before the year, so this fell through to the yearless-date fallback and discarded the real printed year. (2) The page also has an issuance date (\"Date: 08May2026\") and a fare-validity date (\"NVA (3): 31Jul2026\", printed twice) alongside the two real flight dates — four unique dates total, which used to trip the \"3+ unlabeled dates is too ambiguous, don't guess\" rule and made the whole fallback bail out, losing the return date along with the noise. Fixed by (1) loosening the separator requirement before the year, and (2) excluding a date match that has a colon earlier in the same line — both metadata dates here are \"Label: date\" formatted (\"Date:\", \"NVA (3):\"), while the real flight dates are bare table-cell lines with nothing else on them.",
